//[ TurnRecord.jo
  !
  ! One turn as the USER saw it — as opposed to `TurnData`, which is one turn as
  ! the ENGINE ran it.
  !
  ! The two differ because not every turn is a user's. A subagent, a background
  ! job, or a one-shot script all drive `Agent.runTurn`, and all of them write
  ! the same `harpe.turn.*` records into the journal. What makes a turn
  ! user-facing is that a driver bracketed it — `Transcript.request` before,
  ! `Transcript.response` after. Turns without that bracket are machinery: they
  ! stay in the log for auditing and never reach the transcript.
  !
  ! The bracket also carries what the engine cannot know. `request` is the
  ! high-level thing the user actually did, before it was desugared into a
  ! `UserInput` — the uploaded files, the slash command, the form. `response` is
  ! the driver's interpretation of the finished turn. Both are opaque `Value`
  ! payloads: the framework moves them, the driver defines and parses them.
//]

namespace harpe

import harpe.logging.*
import harpe.turns.*

//[ One user-facing turn: its bracket, and the journal entries it spans
  !
  ! `id` identifies this TRANSCRIPT record. The engine's turn has none — nothing
  ! in `runTurn` knows the transcript exists — so this is the driver's own,
  ! pairing `request` with `response` and giving the record a stable handle for
  ! retry, permalink, or delete. The framework never mints one, because a driver
  ! usually has a better one already (a message id, an update id, a row id).
  !
  ! A record is COMPLETE by construction: an opening payload, a closing one, and
  ! the turn between them. `Journal.records` builds none for a bracket missing
  ! any of the three, which is what a process killed mid-turn leaves behind. Such
  ! a turn is not a conversation the user had — it is a fragment — and admitting
  ! it here would put a null check in every consumer to render half a bubble.
  ! The fragment is still in the journal for anyone auditing it.
//]
class TurnRecord(id: String, request: Value, response: Value, data: TurnData)
